{"skill":{"slug":"ssh-essentials","displayName":"Ssh Essentials"},"version":{"version":"1.0.0","createdAt":1769692063680,"changelog":"Initial release of SSH Essentials – a comprehensive reference for secure remote access, key management, tunneling, and file transfers using SSH.\n\n- Covers basic SSH commands for connecting and running remote commands\n- Includes SSH key generation, management, and agent usage\n- Provides port forwarding, tunneling, and SOCKS proxy examples\n- Features SSH config file usage, aliases, and jump hosts\n- Details secure file transfers via SCP, SFTP, and Rsync over SSH\n- Adds security best practices, troubleshooting, and advanced SSH techniques\n- Offers concise command examples and practical tips throughout","changelogSource":"auto","license":null,"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":8364,"sha256":"bb9d9af625b8fdb6ed25a6769956c57ddf40eba66d483b413b0b36de68488fb5","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2229,"sha256":"f1f3c3b6a47781f8e6b39415d51d516cb4eec0b04d5f28b4538a9f78e77ecd71","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789023506048,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"1740cd319b77b309511235a8b0943c907983ec8a15172e2b37e80fd347498343","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/1740cd319b77b309511235a8b0943c907983ec8a15172e2b37e80fd347498343","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"clean","verdict":"benign","normalizedStatus":"clean","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: ssh-essentials\nVersion: 1.0.0\n\nThe skill bundle provides a comprehensive guide to essential SSH commands and best practices. All commands and instructions are standard, well-documented SSH functionalities. There is no evidence of intentional malicious behavior, data exfiltration to unauthorized endpoints, malicious execution patterns, persistence mechanisms, obfuscation, or prompt injection attempts against the agent. The content is purely informational and instructional for legitimate SSH usage.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779160295081},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":91,"severity":"CRITICAL","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","issueCount":23,"checkedAt":1789023506048},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"This is a documentation-only SSH skill, but it includes copyable SSH examples that weaken host verification and broadly expose forwarded agents without enough guardrails.","dimensions":[{"detail":"The documented commands touch SSH keys, ~/.ssh configuration, remote systems, tunnels, and file transfers, which is expected for SSH usage but high-impact if copied without review.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The package contains only artifact/SKILL.md and metadata requiring the ssh binary; no installer, scripts, dependencies, or remote payload retrieval were found.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"ok"},{"detail":"Several directly copyable examples are under-scoped for a security-focused SSH guide, including StrictHostKeyChecking=no, ForwardAgent yes for named and wildcard hosts, rsync --delete, and restarting sshd without validation or lockout precautions.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The skill itself installs no persistence, but it documents user-initiated persistent SSH config changes, background tunnels, authorized_keys updates, and privileged sshd restarts.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The skill's stated purpose is SSH remote access, key management, tunneling, and file transfer, and the inspected artifact is a Markdown guide whose examples generally match that purpose.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"note"}],"guidance":"Review this skill before installing if users may copy commands directly. The main risks are insecure SSH habits: do not disable host-key checking except in tightly controlled throwaway cases, avoid agent forwarding on wildcard or untrusted hosts, dry-run rsync before using --delete, and validate sshd_config before restarting SSH.","findings":"[PE3] expected: References to ~/.ssh/id_rsa and authorized_keys appear in SSH key-management examples, not in executable code or hidden collection logic.\n[T09] unexpected: The static finding about agent forwarding and disabled host-key verification is supported by the artifact; those examples are not necessary defaults and are not explained with adequate trust-boundary warnings.\n[SQP-2] unexpected: The StrictHostKeyChecking=no examples are directly copyable and only one is labeled not recommended, without explaining the server-identity and man-in-the-middle risk.\n[TM1] expected: rsync --delete is a normal mirror operation, but the guide does not emphasize dry-run or deletion consequences next to the destructive example.\n[PE2] expected: sudo appears in SSH administration examples and is purpose-aligned, but restarting sshd is operationally risky without validation and second-session guidance.\n[E3] expected: Listing ~/.ssh is presented as a permission troubleshooting step, not broad local reconnaissance or exfiltration.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789023506048}}}}}